## 86. A single tolerant log-scale alternative and finite Taylor evaluation

For a complete comparison, use the following one tolerant log-scale contract and ordinary proof. An exact true dyadic bin is not inferred from an approximate rate. On a contracting nonzero plateau with q1q2>0 and lambda>0 put u=-log q1,v=-log q2,lambda=u+v. Use one signed integer k and sigma=2^-k with sigma<=lambda<=4sigma. Store nonnegative approximations uhat,vhat with errors <=eta sigma and the same unnormalized three anchors (or paid normalized directions and their explicitly encoded amplitudes). Relative coefficient integers are bounded by 4/eta+2. Zero labels and the tiny branch below use a zero output mode, never log zero. The integer determinant argument bounds k above by log2(N/log(sqrt d0))+O(1). Large negative k does not violate the contract; a tiny gate avoids needing unbounded rates or direction precision for negligible outputs.

The phase coefficient is exp(-2R u-2S v). Along the approximation segment lambda_t>=lambda-2eta sigma>=lambda/2. Its sum of coefficient derivatives is at most 6R exp(-2R lambda_t)<=6/lambda_t, using t exp(-t)<=1. Therefore perturbation is <=12eta. The SAME broad bin gives R>=4*2^k(K+4) => exp(-2R lambda)<=exp(-8(K+4)); a Fibonacci tail cutoff using T=max(1,ceil(4*2^k(K+4))) is legal. No coefficient/tail proof requires an exact true bin. For a head, truncate the rational argument t=2R uhat+2S vhat when t>=K+10; then exp(-t)<2^-(K+10). Otherwise take M=max(1,ceil t), z=t/M in [0,1], put w=p+ceil(log2(M+1))+8 and n=w. Round z to the nearest w-bit dyadic z_w, with half ties toward positive infinity, and evaluate the alternating Taylor polynomial T_n(z_w) exactly as a rational finite sum before clipping it to [0,1]. Convert the clipped value to the nearest w-bit dyadic and raise it to M with the same dyadic rounded powering. Since (n+1)!>=2^n, the Taylor remainder after the Mth power is at most M*2^-n<2^(-p-8). The z-to-z_w conversion contributes at most M*2^(-w-1)<2^(-p-9), conversion of the clipped Taylor value to its powering grid contributes at most the same amount, and the degree-M downward-rounding error contributes at most (M-1)*2^-w<2^(-p-8). Clipping is nonexpansive on [0,1], so the four contributions sum to less than 3*2^(-p-8)<2^(-p)/8. Thus the displayed +8 is a fixed justified constant, with no movable error margin. All rational denominators/numerators in the Taylor recurrence have O(n(|k|+p+log(M+1))+n log(n+1)) bits. Since M<=K+10 on the nontruncated branch and the Fibonacci head has O(k_++log(K+8)) bit exponents, workspace and time are polynomial in |k|+K. Actually constructing a denominator 2^|k| is paid O(|k|) workspace. A negative scale index is not a zero-cost exact real constant.

An all-valid-name acquisition can produce this SAME bin. First request a sufficiently fine finite triple to bound q3^2=q1^2q2^2 by outward rational intervals from |y_i|^2+/-3e. Test the upper bound <=(epsilon/4)^2 for the zero mode; otherwise continue until a lower bound >=(epsilon/8)^2. Every actual q3 satisfies one of these tests after precision O(K), including their overlapping band; ties use <= for zero and >= for the positive guard. In the positive branch each q_i>=epsilon/8. Now bracket their squared norms with rational endpoints clamped to [positive lower,1]. Compute log endpoints by binary range reduction to [1,2] and

log t = 2 sum_(i=0)^(m-1) z^(2i+1)/(2i+1) + tail, z=(t-1)/(t+1),
0<=tail<=2 z^(2m+1)/((2m+1)(1-z^2)), z<=1/3.

Compute log 2 by the same formula z=1/3, with the tail interval included, and pay the multiplication by the O(K) range-reduction index. This gives explicit rational intervals for u,v and their sum. On q_i>=epsilon/8 the norm-to-log Lipschitz bound is O(2^(2K)); at requested b and m chosen so the series/range-reduction error is <=2^-b, the sum interval width is <=C*2^(2K-b). Choose m>=b+ceil(log2(K+2))+16, a conservative finite bound. Use the schedule b=1,2,4,..., skipping log calculation until the positive norm lower guard is established. Stop ONLY if the rate interval [lo,hi] has lo>0 and hi<=2lo. Choose sigma as the largest dyadic <=lo (now allowing signed k); then sigma<=lambda<=4sigma. Refine the norm/log intervals until each coefficient width <=eta sigma/2, round their rational midpoints to the eta sigma grid with fixed half tie rule, and certify the resulting nonnegative errors <=eta sigma. This is a finite guard, not a test of equality to an exact true bin.

Because lambda>=sigma and sigma>=lambda/4, b>=2K+log2(1/lambda)_++p+C suffices for these guards; the final request can be this bound derived from the certified k and K. All valid names in this contracting/tiny domain terminate, maximum precision O(K+k_++1), call count O(log(K+k_++2)), total canonical returned bits O(K+k_++1) using doubling and one controlled final request. The finite logarithm intervals take polynomial downstream workspace/time in K+|k|; true archive production is separately charged. On the zero branch the output error is <=epsilon/4 for every future because q_j<=q3 for j>=3. This alternative substantiates the polynomial log-exp resource comparison. A neutral source with lambda=0 still requires the separate interface of §79.2; no finite-prefix equality test is supplied by this interval guard. The selected construction in §§78–80 uses squared losses and avoids these additional finite logarithm calculations. The exact binomial alternative has its separate exponential-in-p cost and is kept only with that limitation.


